 { In this chapter, we’ll cover various elements of positional piece play, divided into five main sections. The first will cover piece activity (and passivity). We’ll discuss why this can be the main factor when evaluating a position, before showing examples for the related themes of piece paralysis and manoeuvring. The second will deal with piece exchanges, and its relationship to other factors like space, piece activity, and the pawn structure. The third section will be about minor piece play, covering common imbalances like knight vs bishop and helping us to judge when one piece is stronger than the other. The fourth section begins our coverage of one of the more dynamic aspects of positional play: sacrificing! In this section, we'll cover examples of pawn and piece sacrifices and explore the common reasons that make giving up material a good idea. The fifth and final section will continue this theme, exploring what is perhaps the most common type of positional sacrifice: the exchange sacrifice! One last thing before we dive in: piece play and pawn play are intimately linked, meaning that some of the positions covered here could just as easily have been covered in the chapter on pawn play. Take the position in front of you. If the focus is on the knight’s manoeuver to the d6 square, it may fall under piece play. If, on the other hand, the focus was on the weak e5 and d6 squares, then it would make sense to deal with it in the chapter on ‘Outposts’. In any case, these things don’t matter too much, I just wanted to mention it since you may notice that a bunch of the material in this chapter overlaps with others. I hope that this will not be confusing, but instead helpful for reinforcing concepts already studied. } 1. { -KEY- } -- 
